# A failing test is a question, not an instruction

Never edit production code to make a test pass. Decide first which of the two
is wrong, and say which before changing either.

## Establish before touching anything

- Does the test still describe behaviour we want? Read its docstring and name.
- Does it fail on `staging` too, or only here? Only here means this branch
  caused it; on both means it was already broken.
- Is a *sibling* test asserting the opposite? Two tests in tension mean the
  code has to tell two situations apart, and neither test is simply wrong.

## Then choose, out loud

- **Real bug.** Fix the code. Say which shopper-visible behaviour was wrong.
- **Stale test.** Update it, and say what contract replaced the old one.
  A mechanism we deliberately removed is stale; the outcome it protected
  usually is not.
- **Neither.** The contract itself is undecided. Raise it rather than picking
  whichever edit turns the suite green.

## Deleting a test

Count its assertions first. A test that fails on one stale assertion is often
the only coverage for several live guarantees, and deleting it retires all of
them. Prefer correcting its setup.

## Worked example from this repo

`test_compiled_agent_executes_capability_valid_repair` failed after refusals
stopped inviting a repair. It would have been easy to restore the invitation
and go green -- but a sibling,
`test_the_uncarried_refusal_does_not_invite_a_wider_selection`, forbids exactly
that, because inviting a retry is how one turn spent 22 refusals walking the
subcategory enum. Both tests were right. The code had to distinguish a word the
shop covers under other names ("shoes" -> flats, heels) from one it does not
sell at all ("jeans"), which no test had asked it to do.
